\def\title{HTMLDOC}

@s Reader int
@s Writer int
@s Attribute int
@s Builder int
@s T int

@* Introduction.
The OpenResty version of mpresty read its pages with {\it gumbo}, Google's
HTML5 parser, through a Lua binding that offered a small DOM in the browser's
image: |getElementsByTagName|, |textContent|, |setAttribute|, |serialize|.
The \GO/ port reads them with the package \.{golang.org/x/net/html}, which
parses as faithfully as gumbo did but leaves its tree bare: a |Node| has
pointers to its parent, children and siblings, a slice of attributes, and
nothing else. This package supplies the handful of helpers the renderer and
the server need on top of it, so that neither of them has to import
\.{x/net/html} itself.

It also solves a problem that gumbo never did, and that is the main reason it
deserves a document of its own. An HTML5 tokenizer reads \.{<} followed by a
letter as the start of a tag, so the MetaPost line
$$\hbox{\.{if a<b: draw z1--z2; fi}}$$
inside a \.{<metapost>} element loses everything from the \.{<} onward. The
original mpresty inherited that from gumbo, and an author who compared two
variables in a figure got a mysterious MetaPost error about an incomplete
|if|. The part on parsing below tells how the bodies of graphics elements
now reach the engine byte for byte.

@ The skeleton of the package.
@c
// Package htmldoc wraps golang.org/x/net/html with the DOM helpers
// mpresty needs, and parses graphics source without mangling it.
package htmldoc

import (
	"crypto/rand"
	"io"
	"regexp"
	"strconv"
	"strings"

	"golang.org/x/net/html"
	"golang.org/x/net/html/atom"
)

@<Types and constants@>
@<Functions@>

@ Callers see the parser's node type under a name of our own. It is an
alias, not a new type, so the two are interchangeable and nothing needs
converting. The two node kinds callers ever ask about are renamed along with
it.
@<Types and constants@>=
// Node is an alias so callers need not import x/net/html directly.
type Node = html.Node

// Node types re-exported for callers of Walk.
const (
	TextNode    = html.TextNode
	ElementNode = html.ElementNode
)

@* Parsing with shielded bodies.
An HTML parser already knows elements whose content is not HTML. The body of
a \.{<script>} or a \.{<style>} is {\it raw text\/}: the tokenizer stops
looking for tags inside it until it meets the matching end tag. Graphics
elements deserve the same treatment, but the tokenizer's list of raw text
elements is fixed in the standard and in the code, and \.{<metapost>} is not
on it.

So |Parse| takes the names of the raw elements as an argument and does the
shielding itself. Before parsing, each body is lifted out and replaced by an
opaque token; after parsing, each token, now a text node in the tree, is
replaced by the body it stands for. The token is a run of letters and digits,
so neither the tokenizer nor the entity decoder can alter it on its way
through the parser.

A consequence is that entities inside a graphics element are no longer
decoded, so a MetaPost author writes \.{\&}, not \.{\&amp;}. That is what an
author would expect of source code anyway, and it is what the \.{<script>}
analogy predicts.
@<Functions@>=
// Parse reads an HTML document, shielding the bodies of rawTags from
// the tokenizer.
func Parse(r io.Reader, rawTags ...string) (*Node, error) {
	b, err := io.ReadAll(r)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	src, bodies := string(b), make(map[string]string)
	if len(rawTags) > 0 && strings.Contains(src, "<") {
		@<Lift the body of every raw element out of |src| into |bodies|@>
	}
	doc, err := html.Parse(strings.NewReader(src))
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	if len(bodies) > 0 {
		@<Put every body in |bodies| back into |doc|@>
	}
	return doc, nil
}

@ A regular expression finds each raw element: its start tag (with whatever
attributes), its body, and its end tag. The body is matched lazily so that
two elements in a row are two matches, not one long one. Flag \.{s} lets
\.{.}\ match a newline, since figures span many lines, and flag \.{i}
accepts \.{<MetaPost>} as HTML would. An element with an empty body, such as
\.{<metapost src="tree.mp"></metapost>}, is left alone; there is nothing to
protect.

Every token begins with a prefix drawn at random for this call, followed by
a serial number. The randomness is cheap insurance against a page that
happens to contain a word looking like a token; a fixed prefix would make
that a matter of luck, a fresh random one makes it a matter of probability
$2^{-130}$. The function |rand.Text|, new in \GO/~1.24, returns 26 characters
from the base-32 alphabet, letters and digits only, which is just what we
need. (My first version encoded eight random bytes in hex, and had to decide
what to do if the operating system ever refused to supply them. |rand.Text|
cannot fail.)
@<Lift the body of every raw element out of |src| into |bodies|@>=
prefix := "mprestyraw" + rand.Text()
for _, tag := range rawTags {
	q := regexp.QuoteMeta(tag)
	re := regexp.MustCompile(`(?is)(<` + q + `\b[^<>]*>)(.*?)(</` + q + `\s*>)`)
	src = re.ReplaceAllStringFunc(src, func(m string) string {
		g := re.FindStringSubmatch(m)
		if g[2] == "" {
			return m
		}
		token := prefix + strconv.Itoa(len(bodies))
		bodies[token] = g[2]
		return g[1] + token + g[3]
	})
}

@ Putting the bodies back is a walk over the text nodes. The parser hands a
token back exactly as it received it, but I compare after trimming white
space all the same, so that the restoration does not depend on the parser
never merging a token with a neighbouring blank.
@<Put every body in |bodies| back into |doc|@>=
Walk(doc, func(n *Node) {
	if n.Type == TextNode {
		if body, ok := bodies[strings.TrimSpace(n.Data)]; ok {
			n.Data = body
		}
	}
})

@ Serializing needs no help from us. The function is here only so that its
callers can do without \.{x/net/html}. An earlier draft also had a
|ParseFragment| that nobody called and a |String| that only a test called;
both are gone.
@<Functions@>=
// Render serializes the tree back to HTML.
func Render(w io.Writer, n *Node) error { return html.Render(w, n) }

@* Walking the tree.
Everything else in this package is a walk over the tree or a fiddle with one
node. The walk visits a node before its children and the children in order,
which is document order. It is recursive, but the depth of an HTML tree is
the nesting depth of its markup, so the stack is in no danger.
@<Functions@>=
// Walk calls fn for n and every descendant, in document order.
func Walk(n *Node, fn func(*Node)) {
	fn(n)
	for c := n.FirstChild; c != nil; c = c.NextSibling {
		Walk(c, fn)
	}
}

@ Gumbo's |getElementsByTagName| returned a live collection. Ours returns a
snapshot, and that matters: the renderer rewrites the nodes it gets back,
renaming a \.{<metapost>} to an \.{<img>}, and it would not do to have the
collection change under its feet while it iterates.
@<Functions@>=
// Elements collects every element with the given tag name. The result is
// a snapshot, so callers may rewrite the nodes it holds while iterating.
func Elements(n *Node, name string) []*Node {
	var out []*Node
	Walk(n, func(n *Node) {
		if n.Type == ElementNode && n.Data == name {
			out = append(out, n)
		}
	})
	return out
}

@ The text of a node is the concatenation of the text nodes below it, as in
the browser's |textContent|. For a graphics element it is the graphics
source.
@<Functions@>=
// TextContent concatenates the text of n and its descendants.
func TextContent(n *Node) string {
	var b strings.Builder
	Walk(n, func(n *Node) {
		if n.Type == TextNode {
			b.WriteString(n.Data)
		}
	})
	return b.String()
}

@* Changing a node.
Once its image is ready, a graphics node is turned into something else,
usually an \.{<img>}. The helpers below do what the Lua updaters did through
gumbo's DOM. Setting the text of a node replaces all of its children, exactly
as assigning to |textContent| does in a browser.
@<Functions@>=
// SetTextContent replaces the children of n with a single text node.
func SetTextContent(n *Node, s string) {
	RemoveChildren(n)
	n.AppendChild(&Node{Type: TextNode, Data: s})
}

@ Removing children one by one requires remembering the next sibling before
the current child is cut loose, since |RemoveChild| clears its links.
@<Functions@>=
// RemoveChildren detaches every child of n.
func RemoveChildren(n *Node) {
	for c := n.FirstChild; c != nil; {
		next := c.NextSibling
		n.RemoveChild(c)
		c = next
	}
}

@ The parser identifies a known element by its |atom| as well as by its name,
and code that inspects the tree usually compares atoms, which are integers,
rather than names. The serializer happens to go by the name alone, so a node
whose two fields disagree would print correctly and still be a trap for the
next reader of the tree. A new or renamed element therefore gets both fields
set together; |atom.Lookup| yields zero for a name that is not standard HTML,
which is just what the parser gives a custom element.

Renaming is the trick every Lua updater opened with,
\.{node.localName = "img"}: the node keeps its attributes and its place in
the tree and merely becomes another kind of element.
@<Functions@>=
// CreateElement builds a detached element.
func CreateElement(name string) *Node {
	return &Node{Type: ElementNode, Data: name, DataAtom: atom.Lookup([]byte(name))}
}

// Rename turns n into an element of another kind, keeping its attributes
// and children.
func Rename(n *Node, name string) {
	n.Data, n.DataAtom = name, atom.Lookup([]byte(name))
}

@ Attributes are a slice of key--value pairs, and every helper is a linear
search through it. An element has a handful of attributes, so nothing
cleverer would pay.
@<Functions@>=
// Attr returns the value of an attribute and whether it was present.
func Attr(n *Node, key string) (string, bool) {
	for _, a := range n.Attr {
		if a.Key == key {
			return a.Val, true
		}
	}
	return "", false
}

// AttrOr returns an attribute value, falling back to def when absent.
func AttrOr(n *Node, key, def string) string {
	if v, ok := Attr(n, key); ok {
		return v
	}
	return def
}

// HasAttr reports whether the attribute is present.
func HasAttr(n *Node, key string) bool {
	_, ok := Attr(n, key)
	return ok
}

@ Setting keeps an attribute where it was if it is already there, so that
the serialized markup changes as little as possible.
@<Functions@>=
// SetAttr sets or replaces an attribute.
func SetAttr(n *Node, key, val string) {
	for i, a := range n.Attr {
		if a.Key == key {
			n.Attr[i].Val = val
			return
		}
	}
	n.Attr = append(n.Attr, html.Attribute{Key: key, Val: val})
}

// RemoveAttr drops an attribute if present.
func RemoveAttr(n *Node, key string) {
	for i, a := range n.Attr {
		if a.Key == key {
			n.Attr = append(n.Attr[:i], n.Attr[i+1:]...)
			return
		}
	}
}

@* Tests.
\.{GTANGLE} writes these to \.{htmldoc\_test.go}. The first test is the
reason the package exists. Each case is a fragment that broke the
gumbo-based original, together with the text the \.{<metapost>} element
must come back with: a comparison, a string containing \.{<}, an ampersand
both bare and as an entity, MetaPost's inequality \.{<>}, and a body spread
over several lines.
@(htmldoc_test.go@>=
package htmldoc

import (
	"strings"
	"testing"
)

func TestParseShieldsRawBodies(t *testing.T) {
	cases := []struct{ in, want string }{
		{`<metapost>if a<b: draw z1--z2; fi</metapost>`, `if a<b: draw z1--z2; fi`},
		{`<metapost>label("a<b", origin);</metapost>`, `label("a<b", origin);`},
		{`<metapost>x:=a&b; y:=c&amp;d;</metapost>`, `x:=a&b; y:=c&amp;d;`},
		{`<metapost>if n<>2: fi</metapost>`, `if n<>2: fi`},
		{
			"<metapost width=\"200\">\nbeginfig(1)\nendfig;\n</metapost>",
			"\nbeginfig(1)\nendfig;\n",
		},
	}
	for _, c := range cases {
		@<Check that |c.in| parses to one \.{metapost} element holding |c.want|@>
	}
}

@ @<Check that |c.in| parses...@>=
doc, err := Parse(strings.NewReader(c.in), "metapost")
if err != nil {
	t.Fatalf("Parse(%q): %v", c.in, err)
}
els := Elements(doc, "metapost")
if len(els) != 1 {
	t.Fatalf("Parse(%q): got %d metapost elements, want 1", c.in, len(els))
}
if got := TextContent(els[0]); got != c.want {
	t.Errorf("TextContent(%q) = %q, want %q", c.in, got, c.want)
}

@ Shielding must not disturb what it does not shield: an empty graphics
element, the markup around it, and an \.{<img>} that merely points at a
graphics source.
@(htmldoc_test.go@>=
func TestParseLeavesOrdinaryMarkupAlone(t *testing.T) {
	const in = `<html><body><h1>t</h1><metapost src="tree.mp" width="200"></metapost>` +
		`<img src="/source/rgb.mp" width="180"></body></html>`
	doc, err := Parse(strings.NewReader(in), "metapost", "graphviz")
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if n := len(Elements(doc, "metapost")); n != 1 {
		t.Errorf("metapost elements = %d, want 1", n)
	}
	if n := len(Elements(doc, "img")); n != 1 {
		t.Errorf("img elements = %d, want 1", n)
	}
	if v, _ := Attr(Elements(doc, "metapost")[0], "src"); v != "tree.mp" {
		t.Errorf("src = %q, want tree.mp", v)
	}
	if got := TextContent(Elements(doc, "h1")[0]); got != "t" {
		t.Errorf("h1 text = %q, want t", got)
	}
}

@ The last test does what an updater does, and checks the markup that comes
out. The renamed element must serialize as a void \.{<img>}, with no end
tag and without the attribute that was removed.
@(htmldoc_test.go@>=
func TestRenameAndAttrs(t *testing.T) {
	doc, err := Parse(strings.NewReader(`<metapost width="200">code</metapost>`), "metapost")
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	n := Elements(doc, "metapost")[0]
	RemoveChildren(n)
	Rename(n, "img")
	SetAttr(n, "src", "/images/x.svg")
	RemoveAttr(n, "width")
	if !HasAttr(n, "src") || HasAttr(n, "width") {
		t.Fatalf("attrs = %v", n.Attr)
	}
	var b strings.Builder
	if err := Render(&b, doc); err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if got, want := b.String(), `<img src="/images/x.svg"/>`; !strings.Contains(got, want) {
		t.Errorf("rendered %q, want it to contain %q", got, want)
	}
}

@* Index.
